Certainly! Below is a JavaScript program that demonstrates advanced features such as Promises, async/await, destructuring, and modules. It simulates fetching data from a mock API, processing it, and displaying the result.

```javascript
 
export const simulateFetch = async (url) => {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    setTimeout(() => {
      if (url === 'https://api.example.com/data') {
        resolve({
          data: [
            { id: 1, name: 'Alice', age: 30 },
            { id: 2, name: 'Bob', age: 25 },
            { id: 3, name: 'Charlie', age: 35 }
          ]
        });
      } else {
        reject(new Error('404 Not Found'));
      }
    }, 1000);
  });
};

 
import { simulateFetch } from './simulateFetch.js';

(async () => {
  try {
    const url = 'https://api.example.com/data';
    const { data: users } = await simulateFetch(url);

    const processedUsers = users
      .filter(({ age }) => age > 28)
      .map(({ name, age }) => ({ name, age, description: `${name} is ${age} years old.` }));

    console.log('Processed Users:', processedUsers);

  } catch (error) {
    console.error('An error occurred:', error.message);
  }
})();
```

This code consists of two parts: 

1. **simulateFetch.js**: A module that simulates a fetch function, returning mock data after a delay using a Promise.
2. **main.js**: The main script that imports the simulated fetch function, uses async/await to handle asynchronous data fetching, and processes the data using destructuring, filtering, and mapping.